 { Here is another pawn ending illustrating our subject, this time with pawns on both flanks. } 1... { -KEY- } Ka5 $1 { King first! } ( { In contrast, the game went } 1... b4 $2 2. b3 Kb5 3. Kd2 a5 4. Kc1 Kb6 5. Kd2 a4 6. Kc1 $1 ( { not } 5... Ka5 5. bxa4 $2 Ka5 7. Kc1 Kxa4 8. Kb2 Kb5 9. Kb3 g6 $1 { and Black wins } ) 
a3 7. Kb1 Kb5 8. Ka2 { and a draw was soon agreed. } ) 
2. c3 ( { Or } 2. b3 Kb4 3. Kd2 a5 { and the a pawn decides. } ) 
Ka4 3. Kc2 b4 $1 { Immediately striking at the enemy pawns; } ( { whereas the seemingly natural } 3... g6 $2 { permits the unexpected counterstrike } 4. b3 Ka3 5. c4 $1 ) 
4. b3+ ( { After } 4. Kd2 Kb3 { Black's task is easier. } ) 
Kb5 ( { More practical than } 4... Ka3 { when } 5. c4 $1 dxc4 6. d5 Ka2 $1 7. d6 cxb3 8. Kd2 { would let White reach a Queen endgame. } ) 
5. Kb2 bxc3+ ( { Here as well as on the next move, } 5... a5 { would also win, preparing a5-a4 in order to clear the entrance for Black's king. } ) 
6. Kxc3 Ka5 $1 { As we will see many times throughout the book, the king is an excellent performer of waiting moves. Here he is aiming at winning the b4-square. } ( { As mentioned in the last note, } 6... a5 { would also do the trick here, as well as } a6 { and } g6 { . However, in general when we have a choice between a waiting move with the king or with a pawn, the former is preferable since these don't expire } ) 
7. Kc2 Kb4 8. Kb2 a5 ( { Also possible is } 8... a6 9. Kc2 Ka3 10. Kc3 a5 11. Kc2 Ka2 12. Kc3 Kb1 13. b4 a4 { etc. } ) 
9. Kc2 a4 10. bxa4 Kxa4 11. Kc3 Ka3 { Black soon wins the d4-pawn, and the game. } 
