 { Let's see one more pawn ending. } 1. { -KEY- } Kd3 $1 { Of course. White should try to penetrate with his king as far as possible into the enemy camp before taking further action. } Kd7 ( 1... Kf7 2. h4 { gives Black no hope of counterattacking on the kingside, while if } h4 { first, then after } 2. Kc4 { heading for c6, in the upcoming race of passed pawns, White's d-pawn will be faster than Black's kingside passer. For instance, } Kf7 3. Kb5 Kg6 4. Kc6 Kh5 5. Kxc7 hxg3 6. hxg3 Kg4 7. Kxd6 Kxg3 8. Kxc5 Kxf4 9. d6 { and so on. More about passed pawns races in Chapter 2.9, by the way. } ) 
2. Kc4 Kc8 ( { If } 2... c6 { taking the b5-square from the white king, then } 3. b4 $1 cxb4 4. cxb4 { and the outside passed pawn decides – Wedberg. Let's confirm that assessment: } Kc7 5. a4 a6 6. dxc6 Kxc6 7. b5 axb5 8. axb5 Kb6 9. Kd5 { and White wins. We will come back to the subject of the outside passed pawn many times in this book. } ) 
 ( { On } 2... a6 { White could proceed in similar fashion: } 3. b4 cxb4 4. cxb4 $1 Kc8 5. b5 a5 6. b6 $1 cxb6 7. Kb5 Kb7 8. a4 { and the king invades decisively at a6 or c6 on the next move. } ) 
3. Kb5 Kb7 4. b4 $1 { The king can't be improved any more for now, so White starts working on an outside passed pawn. } a6+ ( { Or } 4... cxb4 5. cxb4 a6 6. Ka5 Ka7 7. b5 $1 { and the outside passed a pawn again decides – Wedberg. } ) 
5. Ka5 Ka7 6. b5 $1 ( { In view of } 6. b5 axb5 7. Kxb5 Kb7 8. a4 { followed by a4-a5 a6 and Kc6, Black resigned. } ) 
 ( { Other moves such as } 6. a4 { also worked, but the only winning plan is indeed b4-b5, axb5, Kxb5! at some moment, in order to produce an outside passed pawn on the a-file, which will distract Black's king (a5-a6, concretely speaking) so that White's king can attack the pawns on c7 and d6. } ) 

