 { In bishop vs. knight endings the 'king first!' mechanism is of great utility as well. Here are two old, but instructive examples.The knight is in its element here, untouchably placed in the centre, while the pawn structure makes it impossible for Black to organize any activity at all. But the great knight in itself won't be sufficient to win the game - in order to progress, White should create a path for his king towards the enemy camp. } 1. { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Softening up the crucial e5-square and fixing the g6-pawn as a weakness. By the way, we will see more about fixing pawns in Chapter Four. } fxg5 { This helps the White king's cause; } ( { though after } 1... f5 2. Nf3 { followed by Ne5 Black is also doomed; e.g. } Be8 3. Ne5 Kd8 4. Kf3 Ke7 5. Ke3 Ke6 6. Kd4 Ke7 7. Nd3 Ke6 8. Nb4 $1 { (provocation) } a5 9. Nd3 Bd7 10. a4 Be8 11. b4 { and the a-pawn decides. } ) 
2. fxg5 Bc8 { At this point Black already resigned. Let's see a possible continuation: } 3. Kf4 { King first! } ( { White could also restrict the enemy bishop by } 3. b4 $5 { before using the king; e.g. } Kd7 4. Kf4 Bb7 5. Ke5 Ke7 6. a3 $1 { (zugzwang) } Ba8 7. Ne2 Bb7 8. Nf4 Kf7 9. Kd6 { etc. } ) 
a5 { In order to activate the bishop via a6 d3. } 4. Ke5 Ba6 5. Kf6 Bd3 6. Ke7 $1 { Heading for d6. } ( { Less convincing was } 6. Ne6 $6 Kd7 7. Nf8 Kc7 8. Nxg6 $2 Bxg6 9. Kxg6 d4 { and Black escapes to a queen ending. } ) 
Bb1 7. a3 Be4 8. Ne6+ { This time it is the knight who helps the king to make further progress. Such a combined method is indeed typical in blocked positions within bishop vs. knight endings. } Kb7 9. Kd6 Bc2 10. Nd4 ( { A longer way to the goal was } 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 Ba4 12. Nd4 Kc8 13. Ke6 $1 { heading for the g6-pawn, as observed by Cole3133. } ) 
Bd1 11. Nxc6 ( { In view of } 11. Nxc6 Bxb3 12. Nxa5 { White wins. } ) 

 1-0

