 { In open positions with pawns on both flanks the bishop tends to outperform the knight, and this is no exception. Black's long term task is to penetrate with his king into the enemy camp, thus he first improves this piece as much as he can. } 1... { -KEY- } Kf8 $1 2. Kf1 Ke7 3. Ke2 Kd6 4. Kd3 Kd5 5. h4 $1 { A sensible idea, placing the pawns on squares of the opposite colour to the enemy bishop. } ( { In contrast, after } 5. Kc3 $2 Ke4 { or } 5. Ke3 $2 Kc5 6. Kd3 Kb4 { the black king enters with fewer difficulties. } ) 
Bc8 $1 ( { Preparing a check on a6 in order to release White's opposition, so that the black king can keep improving. As for } 5... a5 { in the spirit of fixing pawns, after } 6. Kc3 Ke4 7. Nc2 { White could try to get in b3-b4 at some point, either trading off the queenside pawns in order to reduce the battle to one single flank (much to the taste of the knight), or creating counterplay by the passed b-pawn. } ) 
6. Nf3 ( { On } 6. b4 { Black plays } Ba6 { anyway, and after } 7. b5 Bb7 { followed by ...Kc5 and ...Bc8-d7, the b-pawn has turned into a serious weakness. } ) 
 ( 6. f3 { preventing ...Ke4 might have been the most resilient choice. As a reply Black could consider } f6 { followed by g7-g5 in order to open a new front on the kingside. } ) 
Ba6+ 7. Kc3 $2 { Understandably, Stoltz wants to avoid ...Kc5-b4 targeting the b-pawn, but now the kingside comes under an attack instead. } ( { By } 7. Ke3 $1 { followed by Nd4, White seems to be able to defend, as observed by Alejandro Botta. For instance, } Kc5 8. Nd4 ( 8. Ng5 $2 Kb4 9. Nxf7 Kxb3 { lets Black reach a mutual passed pawns scenario, where the bishop is much the stronger piece. } ) 
Bb7 { ( } Kb4 { allows } 9. Nc6 { ) } 9. g3 Kb4 10. Kd2 { , keeping the king in the neighbourhood of the knight, while getting ready to meet } Bd5 { with } 11. Kc2 { . With the b2-square available for the king, there should be no risk of zugzwang, although in practical play, Black can certainly continue to torture White here. } ) 
h6 ( 7... Ke4 $2 8. Ng5 { should be avoided. } ) 
8. Nd4 g6 { Covering the f5-square. } ( { The natural } 8... Ke4 $5 { also seemed strong; e.g. } 9. Nc6 Bf1 $1 { (provocation) and } 10. g3 $2 { fails to } Kf3 { . } ) 
9. Nc2 Ke4 10. Ne3 f5 $1 { The pawn advances with the dual mission of dislodging the knight and softening up the enemy kingside. } 11. Kd2 f4 12. Ng4 ( { Or } 12. Nc2 Bf1 $1 13. Ne1 Kf5 14. f3 g5 15. hxg5 Kxg5 $1 { and the plan of ...Kh4-g3 decides - Speelman. } ) 
h5 13. Nf6+ Kf5 14. Nd7 Bc8 $1 { Before entering with the king on g4, Black rules out a knight check on e5. } 15. Nf8 { Thus White prevents ...Kg4 but also misplaces his knight, something that explains Black's reply. } g5 $1 16. g3 ( { Unfortunately for White, after } 16. hxg5 Kxg5 { his knight is trapped. } ) 
gxh4 17. gxh4 Kg4 { The rest is easy. } ( { There followed } 17... Kg4 18. Ng6 Bf5 19. Ne7 Be6 20. b4 Kxh4 21. Kd3 Kg4 22. Ke4 h4 { and Black soon won. } ) 

