 { In this more modern example, the side with the knight could have decided the game in his favour by immediately improving his king. } 1. { -KEY- } e3 $1 { Thus White enables a king journey to b5. } ( { In contrast, the game went } 1. a4 $6 Bc5 2. h3 f6 3. f3 Kg6 { and Black later saved a draw. } ) 
 ( { Changing the move order with } 1. Kf3 { first should also work, since } e4 $2 2. Kf4 { is bad for Black. } ) 
Bc5 2. Kf3 f6 ( { Or } 2... Kg6 3. Ke2 Kg5 4. h3 $1 { preventing the black king's entrance - Braun. } ) 
3. Ke2 e4 { The most tenacious. } ( { After } 3... Kf7 4. Kd3 Ke6 5. Kc4 { Black ends up in some kind of zugzwang, with both the king and the bishop tied to the defence of pawns. Besides, White has the strong idea of a2-a4-a5 at his disposal. } ) 
4. Kd2 $1 { White insists with the plan of bringing the king to b5. } Kf7 5. Kc3 b5 ( { Or } 5... Ke6 6. Kc4 { with a similar situation to that after 3...Kf7 etc. } ) 
6. Nc7 $1 { A useful piece of provocation. } Bb6 ( { In view of } 6... b4 7. Kc4 { followed by Nd5xb4, Black has to pin his hopes on this counter-attack. } ) 
7. Nxb5 Ba5+ 8. Kc4 ( { A good alternative in similar spirit is } 8. Kd4 Be1 9. a4 { , as observed by hong-xu. } ) 
Be1 9. a4 $1 { Passed pawns should advance, as the old saying goes. } Bxf2 10. a5 Bxe3 11. Nd4 $1 { Black isn't even allowed to sacrifice his bishop for the pawn. } f4 12. gxf4 Bxf4 13. a6 Bb8 14. Nc6 { White wins - Braun. } 
